    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d613">

                <pb facs="0852" n="772" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg772"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.4731/196: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Sydney</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Moffat</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sydney</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-11-05T12:00:00+10:00">November 5,
                            1936—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received November 5—11:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your October 31, 2 p.m., and November 2.<anchor n="31" xml:id="d613-f1"/> I
                    delivered your note in Canberra Tuesday evening. It will be considered by the
                    full Cabinet next week when Lyons and several other senior Ministers return.</p>
                <p>Meanwhile I had a long but unproductive talk with Benett.<anchor n="32"
                        xml:id="d613-f2"/> He tells me that Australian and oversea credits problem
                    is potentially as serious as ever and that the London funds have shown virtually
                    no increase despite highly favorable exports. He personally believed that until
                    Australia had adequately built up reserves which would assure her solvency in
                    the event of adverse conditions she could not afford to abandon her present
                    trade diversion policy which to some extent at least is relieving the pressure.
                    He continues to feel just as <pb facs="0853" n="773" xml:id="pg773"/> strongly
                    that we are indifferent to Australia’s difficulties and unwilling to help her by
                    reciprocal trade in a period of stress as we feel that Australia is indifferent
                    to the implications of a policy of discrimination against us.</p>
                <p>The debate on Australia’s trade diversion program which has not yet begun may
                    drag on for some time. Since the recent sharp rise in the price of wool the
                    Government feels that the wind has been taken out of the opposition’s sails.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moffat</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d613-f1" n="31" xml:id="d613-f1-content">
                <p>Latter not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d613-f2" n="32" xml:id="d613-f2-content">
                <p>Presumably Henry Gordon Bennett, member of the Council of the Associated Chambers
                    of Manufacturers of Australia.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
